module

public import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.FiniteDimensional.Basic
import Sheaf.LinearAlgebra.RationalReflectionSimpleBaseOrbits

/-! # Coordinate classification of rational root planes

Source: Milne, *Algebraic Groups*, Appendix C.12, C.14, C.17--C.18 and section g,
table (180) and the four rank-two diagrams, printed pp.610--611, 621--622
(blueprint: lem-sfb-rank-two-root-coordinate-classification).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

namespace RootPairing

/-- A reduced crystallographic rational root plane has one of the four explicit root lists
in an ordered basis consisting of roots. Source: Milne, *Algebraic Groups*, Appendix C.12,
C.14, C.17--C.18 and section g, table (180) and rank-two diagrams, printed pp.610--611,
621--622 (blueprint: lem-sfb-rank-two-root-coordinate-classification).

Proof:
1. Each prescribed reflection is involutive. The group W generated by the reflections acts
   faithfully on the finite spanning set S, hence is finite. Average the coordinate-product
   form of an arbitrary rational basis over W. The resulting rational symmetric form B is
   positive definite: all summands on (x,x) are nonnegative and the identity summand is
   positive for x nonzero. Right multiplication permutes W, proving invariance. Pair the
   reflection of x with the reflection of r, which is -r, to get
   c_r(x) = 2 B(x,r) / B(r,r).
2. Apply lem-sfb-rational-reflection-simple-base-orbits, namely
   `RootPairing.exists_simple_base_reflection_orbits`, to obtain a rational basis D in S,
   integral root coordinates of one sign, and generation of every root by simple reflections
   acting on D. Dimension two gives D = {s,t}.
3. The integers a = c_s(t), b = c_t(s) have product
   4 B(s,t)^2 / (B(s,s) B(t,t)) in [0,4). For strictness apply positivity to the nonzero
   vector t - B(s,t)s/B(s,s). Both integers have the sign of B(s,t), and one is zero
   exactly when both are. If positive, their product is 1, 2 or 3, so one integer is 1.
   Its reflection produces t-s or s-t, contradicting the same-sign coordinates.
   Thus, after swapping the basis vectors, (a,b) is (0,0) or (-m,-1), m = 1,2,3.
4. In the orthogonal case the two reflections change the respective coordinate signs,
   so their orbits on the unit vectors give the four roots. Otherwise their coordinate
   formulas are U(x,y) = (-x+my,y), T(x,y) = (x,x-y). For the positive lists
   P1 = [(1,0),(0,1),(1,1)], P2 = P1 ++ [(2,1)],
   P3 = P2 ++ [(3,1),(3,2)], direct substitution gives:
   U(P1) = [(-1,0),(1,1),(0,1)], T(P1) = [(1,1),(0,-1),(1,0)];
   U(P2) = [(-1,0),(2,1),(1,1),(0,1)],
   T(P2) = [(1,1),(0,-1),(1,0),(2,1)];
   U(P3) = [(-1,0),(3,1),(2,1),(1,1),(0,1),(3,2)],
   T(P3) = [(1,1),(0,-1),(1,0),(2,1),(3,2),(3,1)].
   Linearity gives the negative images. Thus each signed list is stable and contains both
   unit vectors, so it contains all their reflection orbits.
5. Conversely (1,1) = T(1,0); for m=2 also (2,1) = U(0,1); for m=3 also
   (2,1) = U(1,1), (3,1) = U(0,1), (3,2) = T(3,1). The unit vectors occur by empty
   words. Their negatives occur by U(1,0) and T(0,1), and every word is linear, giving
   the negatives of every listed vector. The simple-base/orbit lemma exhausts S by these
   orbits; the reverse inclusion follows because the basis roots belong to S and its
   reflections permute S. Translate coordinates (x,y) to xs+yt to obtain the four sets.
-/
theorem exists_rank_two_root_coordinate_classification
    (V : Type u) [AddCommGroup V] [Module ℚ V] [FiniteDimensional ℚ V]
    (hdim : Module.finrank ℚ V = 2)
    (S : Finset V) (hS0 : ∀ r ∈ S, r ≠ 0)
    (hSspan : Submodule.span ℚ (S : Set V) = ⊤)
    (c : S → V →ₗ[ℚ] ℚ)
    (hc2 : ∀ r : S, c r r = 2)
    (hcint : ∀ r x : S, ∃ z : ℤ, c r x = z)
    (hcreflect : ∀ r : S,
      Set.BijOn (fun x : V ↦ x - c r x • (r : V)) (S : Set V) (S : Set V))
    (hSline : ∀ r : S,
      (S : Set V) ∩ {x : V | ∃ q : ℚ, q • (r : V) = x} = {(r : V), -(r : V)}) :
    ∃ b : Module.Basis (Fin 2) ℚ V,
      b 0 ∈ S ∧ b 1 ∈ S ∧
      ((S : Set V) = {b 0, -(b 0), b 1, -(b 1)} ∨
       (S : Set V) = {b 0, -(b 0), b 1, -(b 1), b 0 + b 1, -(b 0 + b 1)} ∨
       (S : Set V) = {b 0, -(b 0), b 1, -(b 1), b 0 + b 1, -(b 0 + b 1),
         (2 : ℚ) • b 0 + b 1, -((2 : ℚ) • b 0 + b 1)} ∨
       (S : Set V) = {b 0, -(b 0), b 1, -(b 1), b 0 + b 1, -(b 0 + b 1),
         (2 : ℚ) • b 0 + b 1, -((2 : ℚ) • b 0 + b 1),
         (3 : ℚ) • b 0 + b 1, -((3 : ℚ) • b 0 + b 1),
         (3 : ℚ) • b 0 + (2 : ℚ) • b 1, -((3 : ℚ) • b 0 + (2 : ℚ) • b 1)}) := by
  sorry

end RootPairing
